          <text x="100" y="65" font-family="'Manrope', sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#bdd2dc" text-anchor="middle">UTC-4 Offset</text>
          <text x="100" y="90" font-family="'Manrope', sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#36c7b5" text-anchor="middle">Summer Months</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <p class="infographic-caption">Figure 3: Infographic contrasting the offsets and seasons of EST and EDT.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table aria-label="EST vs EDT comparison table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Characteristic</th>
            <th>Eastern Standard Time (EST)</th>
            <th>Eastern Daylight Time (EDT)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>UTC Offset</strong></td>
            <td>UTC-5</td>
            <td>UTC-4</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Seasonal Period</strong></td>
            <td>Winter (Nov - Mar)</td>
            <td>Summer (Mar - Nov)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Clock Direction</strong></td>
            <td>Standard baseline</td>
            <td>Clocks shifted 1 hour forward</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 7: EST VS UTC
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="est-vs-utc" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="EST vs UTC">
    <h2>7. EST vs UTC: Regional vs Global</h2>
    <p>
      Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) is a global atomic reference standard, whereas Eastern Standard Time is a regional civil timezone offset from that standard. UTC is regulated by hundreds of high-precision atomic clocks managed by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures (BIPM). It does not adjust for daylight saving time or fluctuate based on geographic boundaries.
    </p>
    <p>
      In contrast, EST is a localized timezone that covers a specific longitudinal belt. It depends directly on UTC for its baseline, operating at exactly five hours behind it (UTC-5). While UTC remains constant year-round, the civil time in the Eastern Time Zone shifts seasonally. This means that while New York clocks change twice a year, the UTC standard itself remains constant.
    </p>

