  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 8: EST VS GMT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="est-vs-gmt" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="EST vs GMT">
    <h2>8. EST vs GMT: History and Offsets</h2>
    <p>
      Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) is the astronomical solar time calculated at 0° longitude in Greenwich, London. Because the Eastern Time Zone is centered around 75° west longitude, EST runs exactly five hours behind GMT.
    </p>
    <p>
      Historically, GMT was the global standard for navigation and timekeeping, while EST was developed to standardize railway and civil logistics within the United States and Canada. In modern timekeeping, GMT is often used interchangeably with UTC+0, meaning the relationship between EST and GMT is functionally identical to the EST-UTC relationship: EST is always GMT-5.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, a key distinction arises when comparing regional civil rules. While London (the historical home of GMT) shifts to British Summer Time (BST, UTC+1) during the summer, the Greenwich Mean Time standard itself does not change. Similarly, the Eastern Time Zone shifts from EST to EDT, maintaining a constant five-hour difference between the standard offsets (EST is UTC-5, GMT is UTC+0), but changing the relative clock difference between London and New York depending on the time of year.
    </p>

    <!-- Custom Inline SVG: EST vs GMT Infographic (Infographic 5) -->
    <div style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
