      </svg>
      <p class="infographic-caption">Figure 5: Diagram comparing the longitudinal meridians of EST and GMT.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 9: DAYLIGHT SAVING AND EXCEPTIONS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="daylight-saving" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Daylight Saving Time">
    <h2>9. Daylight Saving Time Shifts &amp; Exceptions</h2>
    <p>
      Daylight Saving Time (DST) affects most, but not all, regions within the Eastern Time Zone. The primary purpose of DST is to shift daylight hours from the early morning to the evening during the warmer parts of the year, which historically was believed to reduce residential energy use and support outdoor commerce.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, several territories within the geographic Eastern Time Zone choose not to observe daylight saving adjustments. For example, Jamaica observes the UTC-5 offset year-round. Because Jamaica does not shift its clocks, it matches Eastern Standard Time (EST) during the winter, but runs one hour behind Eastern Daylight Time (EDT) during the summer, effectively operating on Central Standard Time (CST) relative to the U.S.
    </p>
    <p>
      Other nations, such as Panama, Colombia, Peru, and Ecuador, also observe the UTC-5 offset year-round. While they share the same clock hours as EST in the winter, they are situated closer to the equator where seasonal daylight variations are minimal, making daylight saving adjustments unnecessary.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the wider North American context, other states and provinces have unique DST rules:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Arizona</strong>: Most of Arizona does not observe DST, remaining on Mountain Standard Time (MST, UTC-7) year-round. However, the Navajo Nation (which spans parts of Arizona, New Mexico, and Utah) does observe DST, while the Hopi Reservation (which is entirely surrounded by the Navajo Nation) does not observe DST.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hawaii</strong>: Hawaii does not observe DST due to its tropical latitude, maintaining Hawaii-Aleutian Standard Time (HST, UTC-10) year-round.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Saskatchewan, Canada</strong>: The province of Saskatchewan observes Central Standard Time (CST, UTC-6) year-round. Functionally, this means they match Central Standard Time in the winter, but align with Mountain Daylight Time in the summer.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In recent years, legislative debates have emerged regarding the permanent adoption of Daylight Saving Time. In the United States, the **Sunshine Protection Act** was proposed to make Eastern Daylight Time permanent year-round. If passed, it would effectively eliminate Eastern Standard Time (EST), and the region would remain on UTC-4 permanently. Proponents argue this would reduce seasonal depression and increase consumer spending, while opponents (including sleep scientists and pediatricians) argue that standard winter time (EST) is better aligned with the human circadian rhythm.
    </p>

