  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Mistakes">
    <h2>11. Common Mistakes &amp; Scheduling Errors</h2>
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 1: Confusing EST with ET</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> Eastern Time (ET) is a generic category. Eastern Standard Time (EST) refers strictly to standard winter time (UTC-5). Writing "EST" for a meeting occurring in July is technically incorrect, as the region is observing EDT (UTC-4) at that time.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <h3>Mistake 2: Incorrect Meeting Invitations</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Fact:</strong> When inviting international participants, organizers often write "EST" out of habit during the summer. This causes foreign calendar clients to schedule the meeting one hour late, since they calculate the offset based on UTC-5 instead of UTC-4.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

